-- title:
--   Position products of vectors in cyclic word modules lie in the cyclic module of the product
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $[k]=\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}$. A *word* of length $n$ over the alphabet $[d]$ is a map $a:[n]\to[d]$, and $W_{n,d}$ is the complex vector space of all functions from such words to $\mathbb{C}$; it is the coordinate model of $(\mathbb{C}^d)^{\otimes n}$, the indicator $\mathbf{1}_a$ of a word playing the role of $e_{a(0)}\otimes\cdots\otimes e_{a(n-1)}$. The symmetric group $S_n$ acts on $W_{n,d}$ by permuting positions, $(g\cdot x)(a)=x(a\circ g)$, so that $g\cdot\mathbf{1}_a=\mathbf{1}_{a\circ g^{-1}}$. For a vector $v$ of a representation, $\langle v\rangle$ denotes the cyclic subrepresentation spanned by the orbit $\{g\cdot v\}$.
--
--   Let $e:[n]\to[a]\sqcup[b]$ be a bijection, which splits the positions into a first block of size $a$ and a second block of size $b$. For a word $w:[n]\to[d]$ let $w_1:[a]\to[d]$ and $w_2:[b]\to[d]$ be its restrictions to the two blocks, $w_1(i)=w(e^{-1}(i))$ for $i$ in the first block and $w_2(j)=w(e^{-1}(j))$ for $j$ in the second. The *position product* of $x\in W_{a,d}$ and $y\in W_{b,d}$ is
--   $$(x\boxtimes_e y)(w)=x(w_1)\,y(w_2)\qquad(w:[n]\to[d]),$$
--   the tensor product of $x$ and $y$ placed on the two blocks of positions.
--
--   **Theorem.** Let $v,x\in W_{a,d}$ and $u,y\in W_{b,d}$. If $x\in\langle v\rangle$ (cyclic module for $S_a$) and $y\in\langle u\rangle$ (cyclic module for $S_b$), then
--   $$x\boxtimes_e y\in\langle v\boxtimes_e u\rangle,$$
--   the cyclic module for $S_n$ generated by $v\boxtimes_e u$.
--
--   Thus the cyclic module of a position product contains the products of the two cyclic modules. This is used when a cyclic generator factors over a splitting of the positions (such as an inner staircase and a band), to produce elements and constituents of the large cyclic module from those of the two smaller ones.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $x\boxtimes_e y$ is `positionProduct e x y`; the bijection $e$ is an equivalence `Fin n ≃ Fin a ⊕ Fin b` (so $n=a+b$), and the cyclic modules are `cyclic (wordRep a d) v`, `cyclic (wordRep b d) u` and `cyclic (wordRep n d) (positionProduct e v u)`.
